  "nationalForecastSummary": "During the forecast period, the country will be influenced by northeast monsoon, shearline, easterlies, low pressure area (LPA), and localized thunderstorms. During the early days of the forecast period, the northeast monsoon will prevail over Northern and Central Luzon, bringing cloudy skies with light rains over Cagayan, Isabela, and Aurora. Meanwhile, Cordillera Administrative Region, Ilocos Region, and the rest of Cagayan Valley will experience partly cloudy to cloudy skies with isolated light rains. A tropical cyclone (TC) may briefly enter the Philippine Area of Responsibility (PAR). Although it will not directly affect the landmass, its trough may bring cloudy skies with scattered rains and thunderstorms over Bicol Region and Eastern Visayas. The rest of the country will have partly cloudy to cloudy skies with isolated rainshowers or thunderstorms. Thereafter, shear line is likely to bring cloudy skies with scattered rains and thunderstorms over Bicol Region and Quezon. The northeast monsoon will bring light rains over the northern and eastern sections of Northern Luzon and isolated light rains over the rest of Northern Luzon. The rest of the country will experience partly cloudy to cloudy skies with isolated rainshowers or thunderstorms, especially in the afternoon or evening. During the latter part of the dekad, cloudy skies with rainshowers and thunderstorms are expected over Eastern Visayas and Caraga, while the northern and eastern sections of Northern Luzon will continue to experienced light rains. The rest of Northern Luzon will have isolated light rains, while the remaining parts of the country will experience partly cloudy to cloudy with isolated rainshowers or thunderstorms. Northeasterly wind flow will gradually prevail over the entire country. However, during the latter part of the dekad, winds from the northeast to east will generally prevail over Luzon and Visayas, while winds from the east to northeast will blow over the rest of the archipelago. During the first half of the dekad, moderate to rough seas will prevail over the extreme seaboards of Northern Luzon, while the rest of the archipelago will have slight to moderate seas. After this, the northern, central, and the eastern seaboards of Luzon will gradually become moderate to rough. Slight to moderate coastal waters are expected over the rest of the country. ENSO ALERT SYSTEM STATUS: EL NIÑO (Updated: 21 October 2026) A strong to very strong El Niño is present in the Tropical Pacific. Most climate models predict that the ongoing El Niño will persist until the first quarter of 2027, with its peak expected before the end of 2026. Its associated impacts may continue through the first half of 2027. El Niño increases the likelihood of below-normal rainfall, which may lead to adverse impacts, including dry spells and droughts in some areas of the country. However, above normal rainfall conditions may still occur during the passage of tropical cyclones and other rain-bearing weather systems.",
